rs and we'll bring  them um to you as well but look i think  it's important to take a step back and  put this number in some context this is  in roughly the region of 80 percent in  terms of the amount that bills are  rising from what they currently are now  they're currently  just shy of 2 000 pounds a year rising  by roughly 80 percent so that is a  hugely significant rise and compare that  to last winter because remember the  price of the bills now came into force  in april so last time last october the  cap that was in force throughout last  winter i mean we're talking about now  two and a half times as much this winter  roughly uh and so a huge huge difference  for people in their lives in a very  frightening time indeed the causes and  the reasons we've covered them a lot  anna but i think it's worth just  reiterating uh the big driver in all  this is the massive surge in the price  of wholesale gas  those prices of wholesale gas were  rising before the russian invasion of  ukraine but that is the big factor uh  that have caused them to absolutely  skyrocket to record highs in recent  months they really peaked back in the  spring when that invasion happened but  they have started really rising up again  to roughly what they were when the  invasion happened and the reason for  that is there's a a sense and a fear  that lots of companies across europe are  trying to hedge trying to gather in buy  as much energy they can ahead of the  winter but also a sense that russia is  flexing its muscles uh we know that next  week uh the pipeline the big pipeline  that's called nordstrom one it carries  russian gas to europe it's being shut  off for three days the russians say  that's because of maintenance but the  markets fear that it's a threat in terms  of how russia wants to behave in terms  of its selling of gas to europe it sells  some 40  of european gas in the eu comes from  russia it certainly did before the war  so a hugely significant supply and that  is what ca